    "case_id": "71",
    "case_summary": "A 45-year-old male presented with diarrhea and fecal incontinence for 2 months. He also experienced progressively worsening urinary symptoms over 2 years, including hesitancy, frequency, nocturia, poor stream, sensation of incomplete emptying, possible intermittent retention, and urge incontinence. Previous evaluations by Urologists showed no abnormalities on cystoscopy, X-ray KUB, or renal ultrasound. There was no history of pain, lower limb neurological symptoms, or erectile dysfunction. Additionally, there was no history of diabetes, multiple sclerosis, or other neurological diseases. Clinical examination revealed reduced resting and squeeze anal tone, while perianal sensation remained intact. A colonoscopy showed a normal colon, and both fecal calprotectin and stool cultures were normal. Anorectal physiology was not assessed as it was not deemed necessary for management. The patient was referred to Neurology and re-referred to Urologists. One month later, a neurological review noted newly developed mild lower limb numbness when sitting, which resolved upon walking, with no functional deterioration. A full central and peripheral neurological examination revealed only a brisker reflex in the right knee.\n\nAn MRI of the spine showed a lesion just distal to the conus, causing the roots of the cauda equina to be displaced peripherally at the level of L1/2. The lesion enhanced fairly uniformly.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
